(D)  SAMPLING at n = 7, 8 -- is any parity law an n <= 6 artifact?
==============================================================================
  n = 7  (3000 samples) : hp always odd True ; hc even 1645, odd 1355
  n = 8  (3000 samples) : hp always odd True ; hc even 1633, odd 1367

==============================================================================
(E)  WHERE THE ODD/EVEN SPLIT SITS: the directed-odd-cycle census
==============================================================================
  Omega(T)'s vertices are the directed ODD cycles.  hp = I(Omega, 2), so
  hp = 1 + 2*(number of odd cycles) + 4*(...) -- the 1 is the empty independent
  set and is the whole reason Rédei's count is odd.
     n=4  odd-cycle census {3: 2}  sum a_1 = 2     hp = 5      hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 0
     n=4  odd-cycle census {3: 2}  sum a_1 = 2     hp = 5      hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 0
     n=4  odd-cycle census {3: 2}  sum a_1 = 2     hp = 5      hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 0
     n=5  odd-cycle census {3: 3, 5: 1}  sum a_1 = 4     hp = 9      hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 0
     n=5  odd-cycle census {3: 0, 5: 0}  sum a_1 = 0     hp = 1      hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 0
     n=5  odd-cycle census {3: 3, 5: 1}  sum a_1 = 4     hp = 9      hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 0
     n=6  odd-cycle census {3: 7, 5: 7}  sum a_1 = 14    hp = 37     hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 8
     n=6  odd-cycle census {3: 6, 5: 7}  sum a_1 = 13    hp = 31     hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 4
     n=6  odd-cycle census {3: 7, 5: 9}  sum a_1 = 16    hp = 37     hp - 1 - 2a_1 = 4

  The residual hp - 1 - 2*a_1 is 4*alpha_2 + 8*alpha_3 + ... , i.e. the
  higher independent sets of Omega.  Divisible by 4 in every row above is the
  check that the OCF grading is doing what it should.
